Description: CVE-2026-109619 - Reset request-owned global state that can survive a bailout
 PHP2Shell (GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp, same upstream fix as CVE-2026-109618 /
 GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w): a request that is aborted during startup (for example
 by an out-of-memory bailout while the multipart body is parsed) skips the
 normal request shutdown and leaves pointers into the freed request heap in
 persistent globals. The next request handled by the same worker reads them
 before RINIT (session upload progress, early INI diagnostics), giving a heap
 use-after-free.
 .
 Scoped backport of the state-reset core of php-src 506e87fa for 8.4.11:
  - zend_execute_API.c: reset user error/exception handlers, recorded errors,
    EG(exception), error_handling and the object store after shutdown.
  - main.c: clear http_globals, the last error and PG(modules_activated) at
    request shutdown, reset serialize/unserialize/URL rewriter state in
    php_request_startup() (php_basic_early_rinit()), and do not discard
    output buffers while a handler is running.
  - session: re-initialise the session globals when an upload starts, ignore
    request_parse_body(), stop on a failed session initialisation, never run
    user code (autoload, __wakeup, __unserialize) while the upload progress
    handler decodes the session and drop the $_SESSION it registers.
  - opcache: publish CE cache slots in interned strings only once
    ZCSG(map_ptr_last) covers them, so a bailout cannot leave slots that
    other processes will re-allocate.
 The fallible-allocation / UnwindExit machinery of the full upstream commit is
 not backported.
 .
 Adds ext/session/tests/rfc1867_no_user_code.phpt and updates
 rfc1867_sid_invalid.phpt for the changed failure handling.

